{"kind":"propose","text":"PROPOSAL: give a work item two classification keys, initiative and type, and build the reader before asking anyone to write them. Hugh asked for this in session and agreed the shape; this files it for ratification.\n\nThis replaces my 892a1e6e, which I filed a minute earlier with kind \"proposal\". That kind is not defined in this workroom — the defined one is \"propose\" — so it was ruled undefined-kind and carries no lifecycle edge. Same content, correct kind. I am leaving the malformed record rather than retiring it, since it is already inert and a retirement would add noise without removing any.\n\nIt rests on hugh's artifact-path ruling because that is the sibling question: both decide how the durable record classifies things, and both are hugh's to settle.\n\nTHE PROBLEM, IN HUGH'S WORDS. It has become difficult to see \"ADR feature\" or \"JSONata feature\" at the item level. The board shows what a request says and who owes it, but not which initiative it belongs to, so related work cannot be seen together.\n\nWHAT THE LOG ALREADY SHOWS, because this is not a new want. Across 1749 requests there are about eighty-five distinct body keys, nearly all invented once. Three are classification attempts. severity has 72 uses over five clean values — medium, high, low, critical, and one stray \"normal\" which is mine. area has 42 uses over twenty-five values, but they are code paths: internal/custody, ui, cmd/gitseq-mcp. That duplicates what artifact paths already record and is not what \"ADR\" means. priority has 51 uses over twelve values, and it is the evidence that matters: P0 through P3 sit alongside \"feature\", \"usability-bug\", \"usability-feature\" and \"usability-foundation\". People have been reaching for a type and, finding no key for it, putting it in the priority field. The convention this proposes is already being written; it is just being written in the wrong place.\n\nWHAT I PROPOSE.\n\nOne: body.initiative, a short controlled list — adr, jsonata, chess, guard, publication, ci, ui, docs — extended by decision rather than freely. This is the axis that is missing and the one hugh named.\n\nTwo: body.type, one of feature, bugfix, chore. Deliberately not body.kind: the fold already reads Body[\"kind\"] on roster records for actor kind, and kind is the record's own top-level field. It would not collide on a request, since the fold reads that key only when the statement kind is roster, but it would read badly and invite exactly the confusion this proposal is trying to remove.\n\nThree: no workflow axis. I proposed one earlier — review, record, repair — and withdraw it. Two thirds of requests are review work and another eighth is record repair, but that is derivable from structure: a review row has a verdict under it, a repair child rests on a changes-requested verdict. Nobody should hand-tag what the fold already knows, and a hand-tagged copy would drift from the truth.\n\nFour: keep severity as it stands and stop using priority, or narrow it to P-levels only. Two scales competing in one field is how it got into this state.\n\nTHE CONDITION THAT MATTERS MOST. No tag should be written before something reads it. This costs no code to write — internal/workroom/schema.go states that unknown kinds and body keys survive decoding and remain visible, and Body is an unrestricted map — but severity, priority and area are each read by nothing today: statusview/query.go reads only conditions, status, head, commit and artifact, and there is no reference in ui/, cmd/ or docs/. A fourth unread key would be worse than none, because a board that looks classified and is not is more misleading than one that admits it is not. So the reader — one filter in gs status and one facet in the UI — lands first or in the same head, and the vocabulary follows it.\n\nWHAT THIS IS NOT. Not a schema change, not a fold change, not a new record kind. Nothing here needs the fold to learn anything; it needs a projection to surface two fields and a convention page to name the values.\n\nFOR THE RATIFIER. This changes a convention, so it is not in force until ratified, and I am not writing tags meanwhile. If hugh wants a different vocabulary for either axis, the vocabulary is the cheap part and I will refile."}